<Window xmlns="https://github.com/avaloniaui"
        xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
        xmlns:chrome="using:Avalonia.Controls.Chrome"
        x:Class="TanMenu.Wpf.MainWindow"
        Title="TanMenu"
        Width="1000" Height="688" MinWidth="760"
        WindowStartupLocation="Manual"
        TransparencyLevelHint="AcrylicBlur"
        Background="Transparent"
        Icon="/wwwroot/app.ico"
        FontSize="14"
        Foreground="{DynamicResource TextPrimaryBrush}"
        ExtendClientAreaToDecorationsHint="True"
        ExtendClientAreaTitleBarHeightHint="30">

  <!--
    The shell: an acrylic window split into a hand-drawn Windows 7 Aero caption, a permanently
    icon-only navigation rail (48 DIP wide, see NavRailPane below) and a page host.

    Round 3, Task 2 (user items 4, 5) reshaped the rail to match Task Manager's OWN measured shape
    (live UI Automation + pixel geometry, not eyeballed):
    Task Manager's rail is not one flat list — its page rows (进程/性能/…/服务) sit in a top "*"-sized
    group and its Settings gear sits alone in a SEPARATE, bottom-pinned footer group, separated from the
    page list by whatever space is left over. This shell's own 应用/设置 map onto that same shape: 应用
    is the only real "page list" entry (NavRail, a ListBox in the Grid's "*" row), and 设置 — a real page
    here too, exactly like Task Manager's own Settings — moves into a second, bottom-pinned ListBox
    (NavFooterRail) alongside the commands the title strip's old ⋯ overflow used to hold. Round 3 moved
    four of them (打开主目录/刷新/关于/退出); three are still rail rows, because Round 5, item 2 took 关于
    back out of the rail and made it a settings card. A command row is NOT a page — invoking one leaves
    whatever page is currently shown alone — so they are plain Buttons (Theme="{StaticResource
    TmNavRowButton}"), not ListBoxItems: a Button has no :selected pseudoclass at all, which is what makes
    "a command row must never look selected" true by construction rather than by a style rule a future
    edit could defeat.

    Round 4, item 1 deleted the rail's expand capability outright — the hamburger toggle, the 200 DIP
    expanded width, the width animation, the six label TextBlocks and the state class that switched
    between the two widths are all gone. The rail is 48 DIP, icon-only, always; that is both
    Task Manager's own measured pane width and the only state this app ever shipped as its default.
    Each row's ToolTip.Tip and AutomationProperties.Name (ApplyRailText, code-behind) are therefore now
    the ONLY label any row carries, not a duplicate of a visible one. (Round 7, Task 8 is what turns the
    rail into a Windows 7 wide labelled pane; this task does not touch it.)

    ExtendClientAreaToDecorationsHint pulls the whole client area — including the strip where the OS
    used to paint its own title bar — under our control. ExtendClientAreaTitleBarHeightHint is pinned to
    30, the Aero caption band's own MEASURED height (see the WindowDecorationsTheme block below for the
    capture and the coordinates), and it MUST stay equal to the height that block draws:
    WindowImpl.UpdateExtendMargins sets rect2.top = (int)(_extendTitleBarHint * RenderScaling) and
    HitTestNCA derives the OS-level caption/resize band from _extendedMargins.Top, so a mismatch produces
    a drag band that does not line up with the caption you can see. The number appears in three unlinked
    places — here, the root Grid's row 0 below, and WindowHost.TitleStripHeightDip — and
    ShellConstantTests.CaptionHeight_IsTheSameNumberInAllThreePlaces is what keeps them equal.

    There is deliberately NO MinHeight here, and MinWidth stays. Round 4, item 4: Avalonia's
    WM_GETMINMAXINFO handler sets ptMinTrackSize.Y to MinHeight × scale PLUS BorderThickness.Top+Bottom
    (decompiled Win32.WindowImpl 12.1.0), which on this window's style is the full 45+11 px frame — but
    ExtendClientAreaToDecorationsHint has already pulled the 45 px caption INTO the client area, so a
    declared MinHeight="568" forced a 598 DIP client, 30 DIP more than any fit ever asked for, and made
    a bottom anchor computed from the requested height overlap the taskbar. The horizontal case has no
    such error (the 22 px AdjustWindowRectEx reports IS the real horizontal inset), so MinWidth="760"
    stays declared. The app's own 568 DIP height floor now lives in WindowHost.MinWindowHeightDip, where
    it is applied to the request instead of to the window.

    Round 3, Task 2: the ⋯ overflow button that used to sit in the title strip
    (打开主目录/刷新/关于/退出 behind a MenuFlyout) is gone — those commands became rows in the navigation
    rail below (NavRailContent's bottom-pinned footer group). Round 7, Task 1 then removed the strip
    itself: Microsoft's own rule for a window frame is verbatim "Don't add controls to a window frame.
    Put the controls within the window instead."
    (https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/uxguide/win-window-frames), so the search field went
    back to the 应用 page's own content area (Views/AppPage.axaml) where it lived before commit 6d00060.
    The overflow ⋯ button does NOT come back with it — Round 3 Task 2 and Round 5 item 2 removed that
    pattern deliberately and only the search field reverts.
  -->

  <Window.Resources>

    <!-- ══ The Aero caption buttons ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
         One ControlTheme for all three, differing only by width and by the ".close" class. It carries
         the app's own "Tm" prefix like every other ControlTheme this app writes, but it lives HERE
         rather than in Themes/Fluent2/Launcher.axaml because it is window chrome, not launcher dialect,
         and because the decorations template below resolves it by {StaticResource} out of this same
         file's scope.

         GEOMETRY IS MEASURED off Microsoft's own published Windows 7 screenshot,
         https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/uxguide/images/win-window-frames-image2.png
         (Calculator on Windows 7, 278x351, 24-bit RGB, native resolution — self-consistency holds on
         both axes, 228 − 8 − 8 = 212 and 321 − 30 − 8 = 283, and every border is a crisp single
         pixel): the buttons are 19 DIP tall, sit 1 DIP below the window's top edge, and measure
         28 (minimize) / 27 (maximize) / 48 (close) wide — separators at x ≈ 140 and x ≈ 168, the
         close button spanning x = 195..242, against a window whose outer right edge is x = 249.

         The FACE is AUTHORED (Tokens.axaml carries the alphas and says so). The glyphs are vector
         geometry rather than Segoe Fluent Icons code points, because the icon font is the one
         deliberate Windows 11 anachronism this conversion keeps and there is no reason to spend it
         on three shapes that are four line segments each. -->
    <ControlTheme x:Key="TmCaptionButton" TargetType="Button">
      <Setter Property="Height" Value="19" />
      <Setter Property="VerticalAlignment" Value="Top" />
      <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource CaptionButtonFaceBrush}" />
      <Setter Property="Padding" Value="0" />
      <Setter Property="Template">
        <ControlTemplate TargetType="Button">
          <!-- Only the BOTTOM corners are rounded: an Aero caption button is flush with the top of
               the frame and curves away from it. AUTHORED radius. It was written as RadiusControl's
               then-4 minus the 1 DIP border the face carries; Round 7, Task 7 re-valued RadiusControl
               to Aero's own 3, so the derivation no longer produces this number and the 3 is now a
               plain authored literal. It is deliberately NOT re-pointed at the token: the caption is
               window chrome measured off a Windows 7 screenshot, not a step on the app's shape
               scale, and the two happening to agree is not a reason to couple them. -->
          <Border Background="{TemplateBinding Background}"
                  BorderBrush="{DynamicResource CaptionBevelLightBrush}"
                  BorderThickness="1"
                  CornerRadius="0,0,3,3">
            <ContentPresenter Content="{TemplateBinding Content}"
                              HorizontalAlignment="Center"
                              VerticalAlignment="Center" />
          </Border>
        </ControlTemplate>
      </Setter>
      <Style Selector="^:pointerover">
        <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource CaptionButtonHoverBrush}" />
      </Style>
      <Style Selector="^:pressed">
        <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource CaptionButtonPressedBrush}" />
      </Style>
      <!-- Windows 7's close button is red AT REST, not only under the pointer. -->
      <Style Selector="^.close">
        <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource CaptionCloseFaceBrush}" />
      </Style>
      <Style Selector="^.close:pointerover">
        <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource CaptionCloseHoverBrush}" />
      </Style>
      <Style Selector="^:disabled">
        <Setter Property="Opacity" Value="0.4" />
      </Style>
    </ControlTheme>

  </Window.Resources>

  <!-- ══ The Aero caption ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       Round 7, Task 1. Avalonia 12 moved the drawn window decorations out of the Window control
       template into TopLevelHost.Decorations, driven by Window.WindowDecorationsTheme and
       Avalonia.Controls.Chrome.WindowDrawnDecorations. This block REPLACES that theme's Template
       wholesale rather than patching parts of Fluent's, and BasedOn is deliberately dropped: a full
       replacement has no use for Fluent's DefaultTitleBarHeight = 30 / DefaultFrameThickness = 1 /
       DefaultShadowThickness = 8 setters, and GetEffectiveTheme() returns the explicit theme without
       consulting the implicit {x:Type WindowDrawnDecorations} resource at all. The five Style rules
       that used to live here — suppressing PART_TitleTextPanel and PART_FullScreenButton and pulling
       PART_MinimizeButton/MaximizeButton/CloseButton flush to the top — all targeted parts of the
       template that no longer exists, so they went with it.

       WHY THE HEIGHT STILL COMES OUT AT 30 WITHOUT A DefaultTitleBarHeight SETTER: decompiled
       Avalonia 12.1.0, Window.UpdateDrawnDecorations sets decorations.TitleBarHeightOverride =
       ExtendClientAreaTitleBarHeightHint whenever that hint is >= 0, and
       WindowDrawnDecorations.UpdateEffectiveGeometry resolves TitleBarHeight from the override unless
       it is -1. The hint is 30 (top of this file), so the override wins and the default is never read.

       WHY THE UNDERLAY IS REACHABLE BY A HIT TEST, which is the whole point of drawing the caption
       here rather than in the window's own content. TopLevelHost's visual children are
       [underlay, TopLevel, overlay, popover, resizeGrips], and Win32 resolves WM_NCHITTEST through
       PresentationSource.HitTestChromeElement → RootVisual.GetVisualAt(point), where RootVisual is the
       TopLevelHost itself (decompiled: PresentationSource is constructed as
       new PresentationSource(topLevelHost, this, impl, dependencyResolver)). Nothing in the window's
       own content paints in the band — the root Grid's row 0 is an empty spacer with no Background,
       Fluent's Window template marks BOTH its PART_TransparencyFallback Border and its
       {TemplateBinding Background} Border IsHitTestVisible="False", and the one hit-testable Panel in
       that template carries Margin="{TemplateBinding WindowDecorationMargin}", which is (0,30,0,0)
       here — so the walk reaches the underlay and finds PART_TitleBar's ElementRole. The three caption
       buttons are in the OVERLAY, which sits ABOVE the window content, so they win the same walk.

       ROLES → WIN32 HIT TESTS, decompiled from Avalonia.Win32.WindowImpl.HitTestVisual: TitleBar →
       HTCAPTION (2), CloseButton → HTCLOSE (20), MinimizeButton → HTMINBUTTON (8), MaximizeButton →
       HTMAXBUTTON (9). Drag and double-click-to-maximise then come from DefWindowProc for free
       (WM_NCLBUTTONDBLCLK appears nowhere in WindowImpl), right-click on the band gives the system
       menu for free (WM_NCRBUTTONUP is not in CustomCaptionProc's switch), and
       ShouldRedirectNonClientInput returns true for exactly the three button hit-tests, which is what
       turns WM_NCMOUSEMOVE/WM_NCLBUTTONDOWN/WM_NCLBUTTONUP into RawPointerEventArgs and makes
       :pointerover, :pressed and Click work on them.

       WHAT IS NOT EXPRESSIBLE, and is therefore not attempted:
         • Aero's 8 px glass side and bottom frame. On Win32 the only decoration part ever enabled is
           TitleBar — WindowImpl.RequestedDrawnDecorations returns _isClientAreaExtended ?
           (PlatformRequestedDrawnDecoration)8 : 0 against the enum None=0, Shadow=1, Border=2,
           ResizeGrips=4, TitleBar=8 — so FrameThickness and ShadowThickness are permanently zero and
           the :has-border / :has-shadow pseudo-classes never fire. Drawing the frame anyway would mean
           drawing it INSIDE the client area, stealing 8 DIP from the content on three sides.
         • A LEFT-click system menu on the window icon. There is no HTSYSMENU member on
           WindowDecorationsElementRole, so no public role maps to it. Right-click on the band still
           raises the system menu through DefWindowProc.
         • PART_FullScreenButton, which is simply not authored. Every AttachCaptionButtons lookup is
           null-guarded (decompiled), so an absent part is legal.

       THE BUTTONS KEEP THE PART_ NAMES and nothing here wires their behaviour: AttachCaptionButtons
       finds PART_CloseButton / PART_MinimizeButton / PART_MaximizeButton in the template's name scope
       and hooks Close() / WindowState = Minimized / the maximize toggle itself, and it also sets
       AutomationProperties.AutomationId to Close / Minimize / Maximize and marks PART_TitleBar with
       AutomationId "AvaloniaTitleBar". That is where this round's UI Automation verification gets its
       identifiers from — we do not name them ourselves. -->
  <Window.WindowDecorationsTheme>
    <ControlTheme TargetType="chrome:WindowDrawnDecorations">
      <Setter Property="Template">
        <WindowDrawnDecorationsTemplate>
          <chrome:WindowDrawnDecorationsContent>

            <!-- ══ Underlay: the glass band, its bevel, the icon and the title ══════════════════
                 The underlay renders BEHIND the window's own content. That is correct for a caption
                 the content deliberately leaves empty, and it is what makes the band impossible to
                 cover or clip from inside a page. -->
            <chrome:WindowDrawnDecorationsContent.Underlay>
              <Panel>
                <Border x:Name="PART_TitleBar"
                        VerticalAlignment="Top"
                        Height="{TemplateBinding TitleBarHeight}"
                        Background="Transparent"
                        IsVisible="{TemplateBinding HasTitleBar}"
                        chrome:WindowDecorationProperties.ElementRole="TitleBar">
                  <Panel>

                    <!-- 1. The colour tint bar. Aero's user colour was DwmGetColorizationColor — a
                            single FRAME colour — and that is exactly the role restored here, which is
                            why this app's accent survives the conversion with a job instead of six.
                            AccentColor / AccentColorShaded are republished live by
                            Services/AppTheme.cs on every Windows personalization change, so the stops
                            are {DynamicResource} rather than a brush baked in Tokens.axaml.
                            Opacity 0.35 is AUTHORED: the accent keys are fully opaque (they also
                            drive the splash card, which needs them so), and a solid accent bar would
                            hide the acrylic the tint is supposed to sit on. -->
                    <Border Opacity="0.35">
                      <Border.Background>
                        <LinearGradientBrush StartPoint="0%,0%" EndPoint="0%,100%">
                          <GradientStop Offset="0" Color="{DynamicResource AccentColor}" />
                          <GradientStop Offset="1" Color="{DynamicResource AccentColorShaded}" />
                        </LinearGradientBrush>
                      </Border.Background>
                    </Border>

                    <!-- 2. The glass veil — the bright inner reflection Aero had and acrylic does
                            not. Three stops, brightest at 40% of the band's height; the shape is
                            measured and the alphas are authored (Tokens.axaml). CaptionGlassBrush is
                            rewritten by MainWindow.axaml.cs on every IsActive change, because
                            WindowDrawnDecorations has no active/inactive pseudo-class and no public
                            path to WindowBase.IsActive. -->
                    <Border Background="{DynamicResource CaptionGlassBrush}" />

                    <!-- 3. The 2 DIP inner bevel on the band's bottom edge, light over dark.
                            MEASURED: rows 28 and 29 of the 30 (y = 42 light, y = 43 dark in the
                            published capture, whose band is y 14..43). -->
                    <Border VerticalAlignment="Bottom" Height="1"
                            Background="{DynamicResource CaptionBevelDarkBrush}" />
                    <Border VerticalAlignment="Bottom" Height="1" Margin="0,0,0,1"
                            Background="{DynamicResource CaptionBevelLightBrush}" />

                    <!-- 4. Icon, then title. MEASURED: the title's ink starts 30 DIP from the
                            window's outer edge (glyph column histogram, first text column x ≈ 52
                            against a window whose left edge is x = 22), and the icon sits between the
                            frame and the text — so 8 + 16 + 6 = 30 reproduces it exactly. The title
                            is centred in the full 30 DIP band (measured glyph band y = 24..32, centre
                            ≈ 28, against a band centre of (14+43)/2 = 28.5).

                            FontSize 12 is Segoe UI 9 pt at 96 dpi, which
                            https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/uxguide/vis-fonts gives as
                            the system font AND as the title-bar face. It is a literal rather than
                            FontSizeCaption because that token was a Windows 11 optical-size step, and
                            Round 7, Task 7 deleted it outright; the caption's size is Aero's, not the
                            ramp's. It coincides with FontSizeBody, which that same task re-valued to
                            12, and the coincidence is not made into a reference for the reason above.

                            The glow is Microsoft's own number — see CaptionTitleGlow in Tokens.axaml
                            for the DTTOPTS citation. Foreground follows the variant instead of
                            vis-fonts' literal black #000000, because black title text over a dark
                            acrylic in the dark variant would be unreadable and Windows 7 had no dark
                            variant to rule on it. -->
                    <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal"
                                HorizontalAlignment="Left"
                                VerticalAlignment="Center">
                      <Image Width="16" Height="16" Margin="8,0,0,0"
                             VerticalAlignment="Center"
                             Source="avares://TanMenu.Wpf/wwwroot/app-tile.png" />
                      <TextBlock Text="{TemplateBinding Title}"
                                 Margin="6,0,0,0"
                                 VerticalAlignment="Center"
                                 FontSize="12"
                                 Foreground="{DynamicResource TextPrimaryBrush}"
                                 Effect="{DynamicResource CaptionTitleGlow}" />
                    </StackPanel>

                  </Panel>
                </Border>
              </Panel>
            </chrome:WindowDrawnDecorationsContent.Underlay>

            <!-- ══ Overlay: the three caption buttons ═════════════════════════════════════════════
                 The overlay renders IN FRONT of the window's content, which is what a hit test has to
                 find before it finds a page. Margin "0,1,8,0" is measured: the block is top-flush
                 1 DIP down and its right edge is inset 8 DIP from the window's outer edge (block
                 x 140..242 against an outer right edge of x = 249). The 8 DIP inset is kept even
                 though no side frame is drawn, because under ExtendClientAreaToDecorationsHint the
                 client's right edge IS the window's outer edge — the edge the measurement is relative
                 to. 28 + 27 + 48 = 103 = the measured block width. -->
            <chrome:WindowDrawnDecorationsContent.Overlay>
              <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal"
                          VerticalAlignment="Top"
                          HorizontalAlignment="Right"
                          Margin="0,1,8,0"
                          IsVisible="{TemplateBinding HasTitleBar}">

                <Button x:Name="PART_MinimizeButton"
                        Theme="{StaticResource TmCaptionButton}"
                        Width="28"
                        chrome:WindowDecorationProperties.ElementRole="MinimizeButton">
                  <Path Stroke="{DynamicResource TextPrimaryBrush}" StrokeThickness="1"
                        Data="M 0,0 L 9,0" Margin="0,6,0,0" />
                </Button>

                <Button x:Name="PART_MaximizeButton"
                        Theme="{StaticResource TmCaptionButton}"
                        Width="27"
                        chrome:WindowDecorationProperties.ElementRole="MaximizeButton">
                  <Path Stroke="{DynamicResource TextPrimaryBrush}" StrokeThickness="1"
                        Data="M 0.5,0.5 L 8.5,0.5 L 8.5,8.5 L 0.5,8.5 Z" />
                </Button>

                <Button x:Name="PART_CloseButton"
                        Classes="close"
                        Theme="{StaticResource TmCaptionButton}"
                        Width="48"
                        chrome:WindowDecorationProperties.ElementRole="CloseButton">
                  <!-- White, not TextPrimaryBrush: the face under it is red in both variants. -->
                  <Path Stroke="White" StrokeThickness="1.5"
                        Data="M 0,0 L 9,9 M 9,0 L 0,9" />
                </Button>

              </StackPanel>
            </chrome:WindowDrawnDecorationsContent.Overlay>

          </chrome:WindowDrawnDecorationsContent>
        </WindowDrawnDecorationsTemplate>
      </Setter>
    </ControlTheme>
  </Window.WindowDecorationsTheme>

  <!-- Row 0 is a RESERVED SPACER and is deliberately empty. ExtendClientAreaToDecorationsHint pulls
       the caption band into the client area but TopLevelHost.DecorationInset stays zero on Win32
       (decompiled Window.UpdateDrawnDecorationMargins: the inset is only applied in
       _isForcedDecorationMode, i.e. when decorations are drawn WITHOUT the client area being
       extended), so the window's content is arranged over the whole window and this row is what keeps
       the rail and the page host out from under the caption. It must carry no Background: a
       hit-testable element here would shadow the caption's own underlay in
       PresentationSource.HitTestChromeElement's topmost-first walk and the window would stop being
       draggable. The 30 is one of the three copies ShellConstantTests ties together. -->
  <Grid RowDefinitions="30,*">

    <!-- Grid.Column="0" is "Auto", not a literal 48: NavRailPane below carries its OWN explicit Width
         and the Auto column just follows whatever that Border measures at, so the rail's width is
         stated exactly once in this file (Round 4, item 1 removed the second state that Width used to
         animate between; a fixed column here would restore a second copy of the number instead).

         ══ THE CLIENT SURFACE, ROUND 7 TASK 7 ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════
         This Background is what makes the app legible, and it is Aero's own structure rather than a
         compromise: in Windows 7 the GLASS was the window FRAME and the client area under it was
         opaque #FCFCFC. Row 0 above — the caption band — deliberately keeps no Background at all, so
         the acrylic still shows through it and the hand-drawn glass gradient still sits on a live
         blur. Everything below the caption is SurfaceBaseBrush, which Tokens.axaml aliases onto the
         vendored AeroWindowBackground.

         MEASURED, and this is why it is not optional. Until this task the whole client was
         Transparent over the acrylic. Avalonia's AcrylicBlur on Win32 is a plain gaussian blur of
         what is behind the window with NO tint layer of its own, so every glyph on a page that
         painted nothing sat on a blur of the user's desktop. On this machine the wallpaper
         (D:\0.Documents\Pictures\壁纸, 7680x4532) averages (87,84,103) over 120,684 sampled pixels,
         whose WCAG relative luminance is 0.093. Against that surface the 设置 page's group headings
         (HeadingBrush's Light value, luminance 0.050) compute to 1.4:1 and its body ink (pure black)
         to 2.9:1 — against WCAG AA's 4.5:1 for body text. PrintWindow cannot photograph the composite
         at all (flag 2 returns (0,0,0) for a transparent client), so the failure was neither visible
         to the instrument nor arguable away: the group headings really did sample (0,0,0) ink on a
         (0,0,0) background, ratio 1.00, on the build this task replaced. Over AeroWindowBackground
         the same two elements measure 10.59:1 and 20.47:1 in Light and 4.29:1 and 11.90:1 in Dark,
         read off PrintWindow captures rather than computed.

         Border.tm-page-pane:not(.app) in Themes/Fluent2/Launcher.axaml is UNCHANGED by this: it still
         paints nothing, which is still Round 6 item 2's decision that every non-应用 page shows the
         window's own material. What changed is what the window's own material IS. -->
    <Grid Grid.Row="1" ColumnDefinitions="Auto,*"
          Background="{DynamicResource SurfaceBaseBrush}">

      <!-- ══ Navigation rail pane: 48 DIP, icon-only, permanently ═══════════════════════════════════
           Round 4, item 1: the hamburger toggle, the 200 DIP expanded width and the DoubleTransition
           that animated between the two are deleted — a rail with one width needs neither a transition
           on that width nor the ClipToBounds that only existed to clip the animation. 48 DIP is Task
           Manager's own pane width, measured live (its PaneRoot is 72 physical px = 48 DIP at this
           machine's 150% scale) and the width this app has always opened at.

           This literal Width is the SINGLE source of truth for the rail's width. MainWindow.axaml.cs's
           RailWidthDip mirrors it as the window content fit's chrome term and has to be kept in step
           with it — see that constant's own remarks, and
           ShellConstantTests.RailWidth_MatchesTheRailPaneWidth, which is what now catches a drift
           between the two. -->
      <Border Grid.Column="0"
              x:Name="NavRailPane"
              Width="48"
              Background="Transparent">

        <!-- Two rows: Task Manager's own rail (measured, not eyeballed — its NavigationMenu is a Grid
             of exactly this shape) keeps its page list in a "*" row and pins Settings alone in a
             separate Auto row below it, the "*" row's own leftover space pushing the footer down to the
             pane's bottom edge. Round 4, item 1 removed the third (Auto) row this Grid used to open
             with, which held nothing but the hamburger toggle; nothing replaces it, so 应用 rises by
             that row's whole height. Classes="tm-nav-rail" is this element's own name for anything
             that needs to scope a rule to the rail root, and since Round 6, item 3 something does:
             Launcher.axaml's "Grid.tm-nav-rail TextBlock.tm-icon" is what lifts every rail glyph to
             Task Manager's own measured contrast without touching tm-icon anywhere else in the app.
             (Between Round 4, item 1 — which deleted the three rail-state selectors that were its
             previous readers — and that rule, it had none.) -->
        <Grid x:Name="NavRailContent" Classes="tm-nav-rail" RowDefinitions="*,Auto">

          <!-- Page list: 应用 only. 设置 is a real page too (PageHost.Content still swaps between the
               two exactly as before — see ShowPage), but it now lives in NavFooterRail below, pinned to
               the pane's bottom footer the same way Task Manager pins its own Settings gear, separated
               from this list. Padding is "0,12" — ZERO horizontal, unlike a plain ListBox default —
               because ListBoxItem's own Margin="4,2" (Launcher.axaml) already supplies the row's outer
               gutter; adding a second, ListBox-level horizontal padding on top of that (the pre-Task-2
               shape) narrowed the item to 32 DIP against Task Manager's OWN measured 40 DIP —
               confirmed by live UI Automation on both apps. Removing it is what makes
               the item column exactly 48 (pane) − 2×4 (item margin) = 40 DIP, matching
               Task Manager's row width with no other change. Grid.Row="0" since Round 4, item 1 deleted
               the hamburger row above it. -->
          <ListBox x:Name="NavRail"
                   Grid.Row="0"
                   SelectedIndex="0"
                   SelectionChanged="OnNavSelectionChanged"
                   Background="Transparent"
                   BorderThickness="0"
                   Padding="0,12">
            <!-- Every rail icon TextBlock in this file carries a local Width="16": Task Manager's own
                 icon glyphs measure a FIXED, IDENTICAL 16 DIP box at the same X on every single row
                 (live UI Automation on 7 different glyphs — folder, chart, clock, wrench, people, list,
                 puzzle — all report x=5322), which only happens if the icon sits in a
                 fixed-size container rather than being centred by its own raw glyph metrics. Segoe
                 Fluent Icons glyphs do NOT all share the same advance width at a given FontSize — before
                 this fix, "应用"'s grid glyph and "设置"'s gear glyph centred 4 physical px apart from
                 each other (measured) purely from font-metric differences between the two characters,
                 even though both rows' content was correctly centred. A local value beats the tm-icon
                 style's own Setters (the same precedence this file already relies on elsewhere), and 16
                 DIP is not a round guess: it is exactly Task Manager's OWN measured icon box, and it also
                 exactly fills this row's own content width (40 DIP item − 2×12 DIP
                 ListBoxItem Padding, Launcher.axaml, = 16 DIP) with no slack on either side.

                 Round 4, item 1: the icon is now every row's WHOLE content — the label TextBlock and the
                 horizontal StackPanel that used to pair with it are gone, since the rail can no longer
                 widen to show a label. Centring is stated LOCALLY on the icon (tm-icon's own Setters say
                 the same thing, and a local value simply outranks them): it used to come from a pair of
                 rail-state rules in Launcher.axaml that centred the row's StackPanel, and item 1 deleted
                 both along with the StackPanels they targeted. -->
            <ListBoxItem x:Name="NavAppsItem" Theme="{StaticResource TmNavRowItem}">
              <TextBlock Classes="tm-icon" Width="16" Text="&#xE71D;"
                         HorizontalAlignment="Center" VerticalAlignment="Center" />
            </ListBoxItem>
          </ListBox>

          <!-- Bottom-pinned footer: Round 3, Task 2 (user item 4) moved the title strip's old ⋯
               overflow flyout (打开主目录/刷新/关于/退出 — 关于 has since left again, Round 5, item 2)
               into the rail, here — and user item 5 asks to put 设置 where Task Manager's OWN Settings
               gear sits, separated from the page list. Both
               land in the SAME Auto row (Grid.Row="1" since Round 4, item 1 deleted the hamburger row
               this Grid used to open with): Row 0's "*" already gives the page list every
               pixel it does not use, which is what pushes this whole group to the pane's true bottom
               edge without this row needing an explicit height of its own — the identical mechanism
               Task Manager's own FooterMenuItemsHost relies on (measured: its MenuItemsScrollViewer
               fills a "*"-sized 1390 physical px even though its 7 items only occupy 414 of them).

               The command rows — three of them below, since Round 5, item 2 took 关于 out of this
               footer (see that item's own note further down) — are Buttons (Theme="{StaticResource
               TmNavRowButton}"), not ListBoxItems, on purpose: they are one-shot actions that leave
               whatever page is on screen alone, never a destination the window navigates TO and stays
               on the way 应用/设置 are, and a Button has no :selected pseudoclass at all — so "a command
               row must never look selected" (the task's own constraint) holds by construction, not by a
               style rule a later edit could accidentally defeat. This also means they need no
               participation in OnNavSelectionChanged's selection bookkeeping below; Click is the whole
               contract.

               Folding the commands into one flyout-triggering row instead was the other option Round 3,
               Task 2 named — rejected because nothing in the measured reference shows that shape
               anywhere: Task Manager's OWN rail (both its 7-item page list and its 1-item footer) is
               built entirely from flat, one-row-per-destination entries, never a row that reveals a
               flyout of further choices. Such a row would also just relocate the exact ⋯-button pattern
               Round 3, Task 2 explicitly removed, rather than actually removing it.

               Round 4, item 1: each row's content is now its icon alone — see NavAppsItem above for why
               the label TextBlocks and their StackPanels are gone and where the centring moved to. The
               row's ToolTip.Tip and AutomationProperties.Name (ApplyRailText) are its only label.
               Classes="tm-nav-row" below is kept as these rows' own name for anything that needs to
               scope a rule to them, but NO Launcher.axaml selector keys off it: its only reader was
               one of the three rail-state rules Round 4, item 1 deleted. (NavRailContent's own
               Classes="tm-nav-rail" was in the same position until Round 6, item 3 gave it a reader —
               see its remark above.)

               ══ Round 5, item 1: the order below, and why it is what it is ═══════════════════════
               Top to bottom: 打开主目录 · 刷新 · 设置 · separator · 退出. The rows are LITERAL
               XAML, deliberately: rebuilding the footer from a collection would need a DataTemplate
               over a type that does not exist, and every DataTemplate in this app must carry an
               x:DataType (AvaloniaUseCompiledBindingsByDefault + PublishAot, TanMenu.Wpf.csproj), so
               a reorder is an edit HERE and nowhere else. Nothing downstream is index-based —
               OnNavSelectionChanged branches on ReferenceEquals against NavFooterRail and ApplyRailText
               addresses every row by x:Name — so moving a row is inert by construction.

               退出 IS LAST BECAUSE THE USER ASKED FOR IT ("退出移动到最下"). The placement is the user's
               call and is not re-litigated here. Round 4 had put it FIRST, and its argument was never
               that 退出 belongs at the top — it was that 退出 must not sit directly above 设置: 退出
               fires immediately with no confirmation (ShellCommands.Exit's own comment: "there is no
               dialog and nothing to wait for"), and 设置 is the row the app itself treats as
               most-reached, the only footer action with TWO other entry points (the tray menu, wired in
               App.axaml.cs to TrayService; and the 应用 empty state's 打开设置… button, AppPage.axaml).
               That argument's MITIGATION survives the reversal, and is why the separator below moved
               WITH 退出 instead of staying where it was or being dropped: the terminal action is still
               fenced into a group of its own rather than sitting one ordinary 4 DIP row gap from the row
               the pointer returns to at a fixed screen coordinate. A menu convention (退出 last, as this
               app's own tray menu does) now agrees with this footer instead of being overruled by it.

               关于 IS NO LONGER IN THIS FOOTER (Round 5, item 2 — "关于集成到设置页中"). It is a card at
               the bottom of the 设置 page now (Views/Settings/AboutCard.axaml), and the overlay its row
               used to raise was DELETED rather than hidden, with everything it orphaned: OnAboutClick,
               LauncherViewModel's ShowAbout/OpenAbout/CloseAbout, AppPage's About Border, SplashPanel's
               dismiss hint and the ClickToDismiss language key.

               设置 stays bottom-most among the DESTINATIONS, matching the reference: Task Manager's rail
               footer holds exactly ONE entry, its Settings gear, pinned to the very bottom of the pane.
               The commands being in the rail at all is this app's own divergence from that reference and
               was not re-litigated by this item either.

               The separator is not decoration. The flat run of five was an accident of the Round 3
               move: the ⋯ overflow MenuFlyout it came from encoded THREE groups —
               [打开主目录, 刷新] | [关于] | [退出] — and both <Separator/> elements were dropped in
               transit. Exactly one is restored, and only the boundary that carries safety meaning
               (terminal vs reversible).

               Margin="0,0,0,4" on this StackPanel is the footer's own inset from the pane's bottom edge
               (Task Manager's measured ~4 DIP gap between its FooterMenuItemsHost and the window edge).
               It used to be NavFooterRail's Padding, back when that ListBox was the last child; putting
               it on the footer states the fact where it actually lives, so the next reorder does not
               have to unpick it. -->
          <StackPanel Grid.Row="1" Margin="0,0,0,4">
            <Button x:Name="NavOpenRootItem"
                    Theme="{StaticResource TmNavRowButton}"
                    Classes="tm-nav-row"
                    Click="OnOpenRootClick">
              <TextBlock Classes="tm-icon" Width="16" Text="&#xE8B7;"
                         HorizontalAlignment="Center" VerticalAlignment="Center" />
            </Button>
            <Button x:Name="NavRefreshItem"
                    Theme="{StaticResource TmNavRowButton}"
                    Classes="tm-nav-row"
                    Click="OnRefreshClick">
              <TextBlock Classes="tm-icon" Width="16" Text="&#xE72C;"
                         HorizontalAlignment="Center" VerticalAlignment="Center" />
            </Button>

            <!-- 设置: a real page (see ShowPage), styled and measured exactly like NavRail's own
                 NavAppsItem — both carry Theme="{StaticResource TmNavRowItem}", so its selected
                 treatment reads identically to 应用's own. That theme (Launcher.axaml) is where the
                 shape lives and where Round 6, item 3's measurements against Task Manager's own
                 selected row are written down: a 16 DIP pill-shaped accent indicator centred on the
                 row's leading edge, over a subtle rounded fill spanning the row box, and neither of
                 them displacing the glyph.

                 Padding is "0" — not the "0,12" NavRail uses, and no longer the "0,0,0,4" it carried
                 through Round 4. No top inset, because the command row directly above (NavRefreshItem)
                 carries Margin="4,2" and its bottom half already supplies that gap. No BOTTOM inset any
                 more either: that 4 DIP was the encoding of "I am the last child of the footer
                 StackPanel", and Round 5, item 1 put 退出 below this ListBox, so it is not. The inset
                 moved onto the footer StackPanel's own Margin, where the fact it states — the footer's
                 distance from the pane's bottom edge — actually lives. -->
            <ListBox x:Name="NavFooterRail"
                     SelectionChanged="OnNavSelectionChanged"
                     Background="Transparent"
                     BorderThickness="0"
                     Padding="0">
              <ListBoxItem x:Name="NavSettingsItem" Theme="{StaticResource TmNavRowItem}">
                <TextBlock Classes="tm-icon" Width="16" Text="&#xE713;"
                           HorizontalAlignment="Center" VerticalAlignment="Center" />
              </ListBoxItem>
            </ListBox>

            <!-- The one group boundary, between the reversible rows above and the terminal action
                 below. Geometry is in Launcher.axaml's "Border.tm-nav-separator" rule; its Margin is
                 what makes the gap on either side of the hairline (6 DIP row-box to line) read as a
                 boundary against the 4 DIP that separates two ordinary rows. Round 5, item 1 moved it
                 down here with 退出 — see the ordering block above for why the fence has to travel with
                 the row it fences. -->
            <Border Classes="tm-nav-separator" />

            <Button x:Name="NavExitItem"
                    Theme="{StaticResource TmNavRowButton}"
                    Classes="tm-nav-row"
                    Click="OnExitClick">
              <TextBlock Classes="tm-icon" Width="16" Text="&#xE7E8;"
                         HorizontalAlignment="Center" VerticalAlignment="Center" />
            </Button>
          </StackPanel>
        </Grid>
      </Border>

      <!-- ══ Content pane: an opaque surface on 应用, the window's own material everywhere else ══════
           Task Manager's shape: the backdrop shows through the caption and the nav rail, and on the
           应用 page it is covered by an opaque layer. Neither page paints a surface of its own —
           SettingsPage's root ScrollViewer sets no Background at all and AppPage's root Panel sets only
           Transparent — which is what lets this single Border decide the surface under EITHER page
           instead of each page having to paint it separately. (An earlier revision of this comment said
           both were "left with no background of their own"; AppPage's root does carry
           Background="Transparent", so that was half wrong.)

           Round 4, item 5 gave the two pages two DIFFERENT looks on this one Border, and Round 6,
           item 2 flipped which of them is the exception. 应用 gets an opaque pane with a 1 DIP
           top+left hairline; every other page paints NOTHING — Transparent, no border, no corner — so
           the window's own surface runs unbroken from the rail and the caption through the content.
           ShowPageCore (code-behind) toggles the "app" class, and it is "app" rather than "settings"
           on purpose: "on the window material" is the default, so a third page cannot silently come
           up white.

           ROUND 7, TASK 7 CHANGED WHAT BOTH HALVES ARE MADE OF, and the second half's colours are
           now stated at the top of this file rather than here. 应用's fill is SurfacePageBrush aliased
           onto the vendored AeroListBoxBackground — Explorer's own content area was a list background
           — and its 8 DIP top-left arc is GONE: Aero's ListBox sets CornerRadius="0" and a Windows 7
           content pane has no rounded corner, so RadiusPaneTopLeft was deleted with its measurement.
           The other pages' Transparent is unchanged to the letter; what shows through it is the
           opaque Aero client surface this file's own content Grid now paints (see its remark above),
           which is what makes those pages legible and what replaces the Mica-straddle argument Round
           6 relied on. That argument's premises are all gone — there is no Mica, no card fill and no
           SurfaceLayerBrush — and its measurements are archived in
           docs/superpowers/archive/2026-08-04-windows11-measurement-record.md. The
           backdrop-refused fallback is unchanged and still the single source of truth for the
           window's colour (Window.Background, set by ApplyBackdrop); this Border must never grow a
           fallback of its own. Why both looks are STYLES rather than local attributes here is in
           Launcher.axaml's "Border.tm-page-pane" block.

           ClipToBounds — AND WHY THE EVIDENCE THAT ONCE JUSTIFIED IT IS NOW MOOT TWICE OVER. Round 4
           recorded here that a Border clips its child to its CORNER RADIUS rather than to its bounds
           rectangle, on one observation: the 关于 overlay's square accent card traced an arc down its
           first eleven rows on a PrintWindow capture. Round 5, item 2 deleted that overlay, so the
           observation lost its subject; Round 7, Task 7 deleted the arc, so it has lost its mechanism
           as well — this pane's corners are square in both states now and a radius clip and a bounds
           clip are the same rectangle. ClipToBounds stays because it costs nothing and because a
           full-bleed child of AppPage's OverlayPanel (whose ArrangeOverride arranges every child over
           the WHOLE panel rect) would otherwise be free to paint over the pane's 1 DIP hairline. None
           of the five children it has today can: the DockPanel's own children inset themselves, and
           SplashPanel, both tm-empty panels and tm-toast are all centred. -->
      <Border Grid.Column="1"
              x:Name="PagePane"
              Classes="tm-page-pane"
              ClipToBounds="True">
        <ContentControl x:Name="PageHost" />
      </Border>
    </Grid>
  </Grid>
</Window>
